<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kinzua Dam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1965">1965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kinzua Dam is a flood-control dam on the Allegheny River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, completed in 1965. Its reservoir flooded about 10,000 acres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> Nation's Allegany land and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornplanter">Cornplanter</a> Grant, forcing some 600 Seneca to leave their homes. The project broke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-canandaigua">Treaty of Canandaigua</a> of 1794 and remains a bitter memory for the Seneca.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kinzua-dam.jpg" alt="Kinzua Dam" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Niagara · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kinzua_Dam,_July_2015.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1936 a major flood struck the Pittsburgh metropolitan area, and Congress passed the Flood Control Acts of 1936 and 1938, which authorised a dam on the Allegheny River. The US Army Corps of Engineers did not begin construction until 1960; the dam was completed in 1965, and the reservoir went on filling until 1967. By then strong opposition had grown, above all among the Seneca Nation of Indians, and in 1961 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-f-kennedy">John F. Kennedy</a>, citing the immediate need for flood control, denied their request to halt construction.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The dam stands in Warren County, about 6 miles (10 km) east of Warren, within the Allegheny National Forest. It is 1,877 feet (572 m) long and 179 feet (55 m) high, built of earthfill and concrete at a cost of about $108 million, and controls a watershed of 2,180 square miles (5,650 km2). Its reservoir, the Allegheny Reservoir or Kinzua Lake, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> call Lake Perfidy, extends 25 miles north, nearly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. The Corps of Engineers credits it with avoiding an estimated $247 million of flood damage during tropical storm Agnes in 1972.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The land taken was nearly one third of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allegany-reservation">Allegany Reservation</a>, granted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-canandaigua">Treaty of Canandaigua</a>, which President Washington signed, and much of its fertile farmland. The displaced Seneca were resettled in two new communities with modern amenities, Jimersontown and an area south of Steamburg, and this forced modernisation is one source of their lasting resentment. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> most of the Cornplanter Tract was condemned, and the cemetery holding Cornplanter and 300 of his descendants and followers was moved to a new site west of Bradford. Several communities in the floodplain, among them Kinzua and Quaker Bridge, lost their homes and their township governments.</p><p>The loss is the subject of Peter La Farge's song As Long as the Grass Shall Grow, recorded by Johnny Cash on his 1964 album Bitter Tears: Ballads of the American Indian, and of a verse of Buffy Sainte-Marie's 1965 song Now That The Buffalo's Gone, which says the treaty is being broken by Kinzua Dam. A documentary for PBS, Lake of Betrayal, was released in October 2017.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kinzua Dam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the United States the nations resisted termination in the 1950s and 1960s, and no New York tribe was terminated, though the <b>Kinzua Dam</b> dislocated about 500 Seneca.</blockquote><a href="/culture/haudenosaunee">Haudenosaunee · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Kinzua Dam</b> flooded Allegany land in the 1960s.</blockquote><a href="/state/seneca-nation">Seneca Nation</a></li></ul></section>
